# Simplified JIRA Ticket Format

<introduction>
This reference documents the simplified format for creating JIRA tickets within this project. It provides a standardized structure for ticket creation that ensures consistency, readability, and focuses on essential information needed for implementation.
</introduction>

<context>
- Creating new JIRA tickets for features or bugs
- Documenting requirements in JIRA-compatible format
- Converting feature specifications to implementable JIRA tasks
- Maintaining consistent ticket structure across teams
</context>

<format_principles>
- Use plain text format without complex Markdown
- Include only essential JIRA fields relevant to the task
- Use consistent list formatting with specific markers
- Apply clear section headers with standardized separators
- Maintain minimal but sufficient detail for implementation
- Follow organizational naming and classification standards
</format_principles>

<required_fields>
- JIRA ID and descriptive title on first line
- Type classification (Bug, Feature, Task, etc.)
- Priority level (High, Medium, Low)
- Components affected (comma-separated list)
- Description section with concise overview
- Requirements section with bulleted list using asterisks
- Technical Details section when implementation needs clarification
- Configuration section for environment-specific settings
- Acceptance Criteria as bulleted list using hyphens
- Estimated Time in hours for planning purposes
</required_fields>

<formatting_standards>
- Use plain text without fancy formatting
- Section headers in title case followed by colons
- Asterisks (*) for requirements list items
- Hyphens (-) for acceptance criteria items
- Single blank line between sections
- No checkboxes, code blocks, or complex Markdown syntax
- Consistent indentation for all list items
</formatting_standards>

<warning>
Inconsistent ticket formatting leads to confusion, missed requirements, and implementation errors. Following the standardized format ensures that all stakeholders have a common understanding of what is being requested and how completion will be verified.
</warning>

<examples>
<example>
MTCNCT-123: Implement Tool Offset Feature

Type: Feature
Priority: High
Components: MTConnect Adapter, Integration

Description:
Implement tool offset feature using MEL API.

Requirements:
* Create ToolOffset class
* Integrate with DevicePath class
* Support multiple tool offsets

Technical Details:
Use MEL API:
* SECT_T_OFFSET_NUMBER_OF_SETS for count
* SECT_T_OFFSET_LENGTH_X/Y/Z for geometry

Acceptance Criteria:
- Data correctly read from CNC
- Data exposed through adapter
- Feature configurable

Estimated Time: 40 hours
</example>

<example type="alternative">
MTCNCT-456: Fix Connection Timeout Bug

Type: Bug
Priority: Critical
Components: Network, Connection Management

Description:
Connection to CNC fails with timeout when network latency is high.

Requirements:
* Increase timeout threshold
* Add retry logic
* Log detailed connection errors

Acceptance Criteria:
- Connection succeeds with up to 500ms latency
- System automatically retries 3 times before failure
- Error logs contain sufficient information for diagnosis

Estimated Time: 16 hours
</example>
</examples>

<critical_points>
- NEVER use Markdown headers (# or ## symbols) in ticket content
- ALWAYS use section names with standard formatting (Name: Content)
- USE asterisks (*) consistently for requirements list items
- USE hyphens (-) consistently for acceptance criteria items
- INCLUDE essential sections (Description, Requirements, Acceptance Criteria)
- SEPARATE sections with a single blank line for readability
- OMIT unnecessary fields unless specifically requested
- MAINTAIN consistent structure across all tickets
- PROVIDE sufficient technical detail for implementation
- AVOID overly complex formatting that may break in JIRA
</critical_points>